33df1c9d9b69"],

Cell["\<\
The raw FeynCalc output is mathematically equivalent to the final analytical \
expression presented in the text. However, because FeynCalc automatically \
evaluates the amplitude in the fully symmetric on-shell limit (p1^2 = p2^2 = \
MT^2), recovering the exact bracketed structure requires applying four \
specific kinematic and algebraic relations:

1. Passarino-Veltman Kinematic Symmetries
Because the incoming and outgoing top quarks are on-shell and share the exact \
same mass, the loop integrals are fully symmetric under the exchange of p1 \
and p2. Applying this symmetry yields:

C2 -> C1

C22 -> C11

This explains why the raw output groups the three-matrix chain into (C0 + \
2C1) rather than (C0 + C1 + C2), and why C11 appears factored with both \
p1^[Mu] and p2^[Mu] instead of mixing with C22.

2. The Dirac Reversal Identity
To group the unsimplified chain -(p1_slash) [Gamma]^[Mu] (p2_slash) \
Overscript[[Gamma], _]^6 C1 into the (p2_slash) [Gamma]^[Mu] (p1_slash) and \
individual vector terms seen in the analytical expression, we apply the \
standard Dirac reversal identity:

(p1_slash) [Gamma]^[Mu] (p2_slash) = (p2_slash) [Gamma]^[Mu] (p1_slash) - 2 \
p1^[Mu] (p2_slash) - 2 p2^[Mu] (p1_slash) + 2(p1.p2) [Gamma]^[Mu]

When substituted, the -2 p1^[Mu] (p2_slash) and -2 p2^[Mu] (p1_slash) terms \
perfectly cancel the stray C1 coefficients mixed inside the C12 brackets, \
leaving exactly -2 p2^[Mu] C12 and -2 p1^[Mu] C12.

3. Mandelstam Expansion\[LineSeparator]
The reversal identity generates a residual +2(p1.p2) [Gamma]^[Mu] P_R C1 \
term. By applying the on-shell Mandelstam relation:

2(p1.p2) = s - 2 MT^2

this residual expands into s [Gamma]^[Mu] P_R C1 - 2 MT^2 [Gamma]^[Mu] P_R \
C1. When grouped with the existing [Gamma]^[Mu] P_R terms, it yields the \
exact +4 MT^2 C1 coefficient present in the final expression (which was \
originally written as 2 p1^2 C1 + 2 p2^2 C2).

4. Chiral Projector Collapse
The raw output ends with + 2 [Delta]CTL [Gamma]^[Mu] (1 - Overscript[[Gamma], \
_]^6). Substituting the right-handed projector (Overscript[[Gamma], _]^6 = \
P_R) collapses the term directly into the left-handed state:

[Gamma]^[Mu] (1 - P_R) = [Gamma]^[Mu] P_L

yielding exactly + 2 [Delta]L [Gamma]^[Mu] P_L.\[LineSeparator]\
\[LineSeparator]\[LineSeparator]\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.99788247258913*^9, 3.997882503776081*^9}, {
   3.997882592405572*^9, 3.9978825936437674`*^9}, 
   3.997882623735094*^9},ExpressionUUID->"18f67291-536a-43c8-aec0-\
8eec0db9c9cc"],

